    "seconds": 168,
    "summary": "The anomaly is mitigated. The cause was the `kafkaQueueProblems` fault injection, which was enabled by default in the flagd config.\n\n**What I found**\n- `kafkaQueueProblems` had `defaultVariant: \"on\"` in the `flagd-config` ConfigMap. Its description says it overloads the Kafka queue and adds a consumer-side delay. Upstream ships it as `off`.\n- Kafka was OOMKilled at <time> (700Mi limit, 400M heap), and `fraud-detection` was OOMKilled at <time>. Both were restarting under the Kafka overload.\n- Other symptoms were not current: the recommendation \"connection refused\" errors predate `product-catalog` becoming ready, and the `ad` OOM was a one-time startup event.\n\n**What I changed**\n- Set `kafkaQueueProblems.defaultVariant` to `\"off\"` in the `flagd-config` ConfigMap. This was the only change to the flag file.\n- Ran `kubectl rollout restart deploy/flagd` so the init container re-copies the file. The new flagd pod's init log shows `\"defaultVariant\": \"off\"`.\n- The original file is saved at `/tmp/flagd-fix/original.flagd.json` if you need to revert.\n\n**Verification**\n- Kafka and `fraud-detection` have not restarted since the flag change; their last restarts are from before it. `accounting` is consuming orders.\n- I could not query OFREP directly because the image has no shell and the workstation has no `curl` or `python3`. The init-container output is the evidence I have.\n\n**Follow-ups**\n- Anyone using flagd-ui to flip flags will lose those changes on the next flagd restart, because the ConfigMap is the source of truth. I did not check whether anyone set `kafkaQueueProblems` through the UI.\n- If the `ad` service OOMs again, its 300Mi limit with `-Xmx200m` may need more headroom. That is out of scope here.",
